At a coming-of-age ceremony in a close-knit community, a young adult publicly declines to participate in a long-standing religious ritual—such as a vow of obedience or a gender-specific role—saying it feels outdated and conflicts with their personal sense of fairness. Community elders express concern that such refusals weaken shared values and disrespect tradition, while others support the individual’s right to follow their own conscience.

How should the community respond to this refusal?:
"A" = Support their refusal and use this moment to reconsider whether the custom should continue at all.
"B" = Allow the individual to opt out this time, but keep the custom in place for others who value it.
"C" = Respect their choice and open a dialogue about updating the custom to better reflect contemporary values.
"D" = Uphold the custom as essential; the individual should be gently but firmly encouraged to comply to preserve tradition and unity.
"E" = Encourage participation while acknowledging their concerns, perhaps offering a modified but still recognizable version of the ritual.

